# This CITATION.cff file describes how to cite this software.
# NOTE: the author list is anonymized while the associated article is under
# double-blind review. Replace the placeholder author with the real author
# list upon acceptance, and fill in the Zenodo DOI after the first release
# (see README, section "Linking this repository to Zenodo").
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the
  metadata from this file.
title: >-
  baboon-acoustics: formant, F0, glottal-source and
  vocal-tract-length analysis tools
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Anonymized for review"
    website: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20764395"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.XXXXXXX"
    description: Versioned DOI of this software deposit (to be filled after the first Zenodo release)
repository-code: "https://github.com/<your-account>/baboon-acoustics"
abstract: >-
  Python tools for the acoustic analysis of baboon vocalizations:
  formant tracking with Praat's Burg method and with iterative adaptive
  inverse filtering (IAIF), fundamental-frequency estimation including a
  Gaussian pre-filtered autocorrelation mode, vocal-tract-length
  estimation after Reby & McComb (2003), and glottal-source analysis
  (single pulse and full pulse train). Supplementary code of the article
  "Re-analysis of formant trajectories in a chacma baboon wahoo
  vocalization" (anonymized for review).
keywords:
  - bioacoustics
  - formants
  - vocal tract length
  - fundamental frequency
  - IAIF
  - Praat
  - Parselmouth
  - baboon
license: MIT
